title: Operation dependencies
tags: [[Revert]]
kind: reference
summary: A set of operations can be reverted when nothing outside it, since its oldest member, wrote one of its cells; otherwise it is blocked — and its dependency closure is what --with-dependents reverts.

Every operation writes one ''cell'':

* an operation with a field name writes `(metarecord, field)` — the watcher's `file_*` operations included, each naming the field it wrote;
* `create_metarecord`, `delete_metarecord` and `set_metarecord` write the ''whole metarecord'': `(metarecord, *)`, which intersects every cell of it.

Operations whose cells do not intersect are ''independent'': they commute. Sharing a
cell, they do not — a `set_field` replaces every row of its cell, so the later one erases
what the earlier wrote. Hence the rule:

''A set of operations is revertable when no operation outside it, between its oldest
member and HEAD along HEAD's ancestry, writes one of its cells.'' Otherwise it is
''blocked by'' that operation, which the daemon names — so "why can I not undo this?" is
answered by an operation you can go and look at, and usually revert first.

The check is ''structural'': it reads the log and nothing else — not the files, not the
values, not even the snapshots. Two callers at the same HEAD get the same answer, until
HEAD moves. It is cheap: per metarecord the set touches, a prefix read of that
metarecord's operations — proportional to their history, not to the log.

''Rollback needs no such check'': it undoes operations newest first down to its target, so
whatever overwrote an operation is undone before that operation's turn. The ordering gives
it structurally what a revert must check. Dependency is also a separate question from
whether the ''file'' side can be undone ([[Revisions and operations]]).

!! Taking the blockers along

`with_dependents` reverts the ''dependency closure'': add every blocker to the set,
recompute, repeat until nothing is added. One pass is not enough — an entity-wide blocker
widens the cells in play (a `delete_metarecord` dragged in makes every operation on that
metarecord a blocker in turn). It terminates, a blocker always being younger than the
set's oldest member. The closure need not be contiguous: operations writing none of its
cells stay between its members, untouched.

Look at how far it grew before confirming. At the limit, where it swallows everything
since the target, a dependent-including revert ''is'' a rollback to the target's parent,
written forward instead of moving HEAD. Between "undo this one field" and "undo
everything since", the mechanism is continuous; the closure's size says where a request
landed.

A target must lie on HEAD's ancestry: an operation on a branch a rollback abandoned is not
part of the current history (`400`).
